# 0325 — Departures, edges, and acknowledgements

**Version:** `v0.14.0`
**Kind:** breaking (toast lifecycle signatures, one removed helper) + additive

Plan 014 left four motion seams open and plan 021 left one. All five are
closed here: three built, two decided against with the measurement that
settles them.

## 1. A toast leaves instead of vanishing

```rust
// Before
state.advance(tick);
queue.advance(tick);
state.is_visible(tick)

// After — the tier travels with the frame
state.advance(tick, system.motion);
queue.advance(tick, system.motion);
state.is_visible(tick, system.motion)
```

`ToastState::advance` passed `MotionPolicy::Off` to `Presence::advance`, which
takes the instant-hide branch. `PresencePhase::Exiting` was therefore
**structurally unreachable** for toasts no matter what the design system said —
the exit phase shipped in migration 0289 and nothing could ever enter it.

`Presence::toast` and `Presence::persistent` now carry a `TOAST_EXIT_MS`
(120 ms) exit, and a leaving toast holds its row while it fades, so the toasts
beneath it do not jump to fill a gap that is still occupied. `ToastState::is_leaving`
reports the phase. `MotionPolicy::Off` collapses it back to an instant hide.

`paint_alpha` checks the exit **first**: a leaving toast has no `shown_at`
(presence has left `Visible`), so every arrival rule below it — including "an
error never fades in" — had nothing to measure from and returned `1.0`. An
error still arrives at full strength; it fades on the way out like anything
else, because "read me now" is not "stay after you are gone".

## 2. A scrolled region says it continues

`scroll::paint_scroll_edges` is the continuation-cue authority.
`scroll::paint_scrolled_region(buffer, content, gutter, total, viewport, offset, system)`
combines it with the standard list scrollbar. `Tree`, `Select`, `Viewport`,
`CompletionMenu` and `Form` use the combined call; `List` and `ScrollArea`
pair the edge authority with `render_scrollbar` because they supply custom
`ScrollbarSpec`s. `design_gate.rs::a_scrolled_region_says_it_continues` fails
the build when any widget paints either scrollbar path without the edge
authority.

A scrollbar answers *where you are*. It does not answer whether the row under
your eye is the end of the content or the end of the pane. The fade answers
that in the one channel a terminal has resolution to spare in — colour.

The helper subtracts the gutter from the faded columns itself, so no caller can
dim the scrollbar thumb at the very edge the thumb is reporting on.

## 3. A copy says it happened

`style::ActionFlash` is the shared acknowledgement: `fire(elapsed_ms)`,
`is_lit`, `alpha`, `next_deadline_ms`, over `ACTION_FLASH_MS` (1 s). Wired into
`CodeBlockState::copied` (mark at the header's trailing edge) and
`SourceCitationState::copied`.

Nothing on screen changes when text reaches the clipboard, so without a mark
the operator cannot tell a successful copy from a swallowed keystroke and
presses again. Reduced motion **keeps** the mark and drops only the fade: the
mark is a statement of fact, not an animation.

A copy site with a header or chip flashes there. A site with nowhere to put a
mark returns its `Copy` outcome and the host raises a toast — that is the host's
effect to own, not the widget's.

## 4. A running step breathes like a running status

`TimelineStatus::channel()` returns `MotionChannel::Live` for `Running`,
`Wait` for `Pending`, and `Static` for every terminal state — the same channels
and periods `SemanticStatus::channel()` already uses. The marker cell breathes;
the label never does. Plan 014 called the running rail "a design call about the
accent budget"; it needs no new wave, because a running step and a running
status indicator are the same fact and now say so identically.

## 5. Two things deliberately not built

**The scroll `target_offset`/`display_offset` tween.** Plan 014 deferred it as
blocked on a 16 ms clock; that blocker is gone (`DesignSystem::at(tick)` reaches
every widget). It is not built because it cannot work: terminal scroll position
is **cell-quantised**. A display offset lagging the target renders only at whole
rows, so an 80–200 ms ease produces *nothing, then a jump, then a jump* — delay,
not smoothing. A colour cross-fade has 24 bits of resolution to travel through;
a row position has one. `WheelAccumulator` fails the same test: crossterm's
wheel events are already row-quantised and the runtime already coalesces
redraws per frame, so there is nothing sub-row to accumulate.

`scroll_ease_duration`, `SCROLL_EASE_MIN` and `SCROLL_EASE_MAX` are **removed**.
They were public API with zero consumers whose only purpose was timing that
tween.

**The collections filter fade.** Fading rows in after a filter edit dims the
list during exactly the keystrokes the operator is reading it to choose the
next one. The count and the query already ride `PanelTitleSpec` (`[7] /err`),
which states the change without hiding the content.

## Consumer edits

```rust
// Toast lifecycle takes the tier.
queue.advance(tick, system.motion);
if state.is_visible(tick, system.motion) { /* … */ }

// Scrolled surfaces call one function.
termrock::scroll::paint_scrolled_region(buffer, body, gutter, total, viewport, offset, system);

// Copy sites stamp the acknowledgement.
state.copied.fire(system.elapsed_ms());
```

